WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:05.180
This video describes
a device that

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:08.750
can capture the human figure
through walls using RF signals.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:10.655
We place our device
behind a wall,

00:00:10.655 --> 00:00:12.530
and it can see the
silhouette of a person who

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:14.550
walks in an adjacent room.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.680
The device works
correctly even if the room

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.200
is completely closed.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.130
For example, if a person
stands behind the wall,

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:23.420
the device's output
looks like this.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.520
In particular, the
output on the right

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:27.260
shows the background
in navy blue

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.320
and the various human body parts
in red, orange, and yellow.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.940
Here, we can see the person's
head, chest, arms, and feet.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:36.720
How does this work?

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.000
The device operates by
transmitting wireless signals

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.630
that traverse the wall,
reflect off the human body,

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:42.540
and come back.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:45.980
At every point in time, only
a subset of human body parts

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.920
reflect the signal
back to the device.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:53.150
Here, we show the output of
a device as the person walks.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:55.130
At different points in
time, different parts

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:57.050
of the human body
reflect the signal,

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:59.117
and the device captures
multiple snapshots

00:00:59.117 --> 00:01:00.200
at various points in time.

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:05.930
It then combines the snapshots
through a reconstruction

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:08.840
algorithm that allows the device
to recover the human silhouette

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:10.262
through the wall.

00:01:10.262 --> 00:01:14.990
Here, we can see the person's
head, chest, arms, and feet.

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:17.300
The device can distinguish
between different people

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:18.290
behind the wall.

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:20.960
So for example over here, we
asked two different people

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:22.440
to stand behind the wall.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.430
And this is the
output of our device.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:26.300
By training on
different subjects,

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:29.600
we can use a classifier to
distinguish between them.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.700
The device can also distinguish
between certain human postures.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.350
If the person stands straight,
the output looks like this.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:40.150
Well, when someone
stands in other postures,

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:44.060
the device's output
reflects his postures.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:47.750
The device can also track human
limbs from behind the wall.

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:51.770
Here, we show a scenario where a
person draws a shape in the air

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:54.640
and we show the output of
our device to the right.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:56.840
The device can trace
the person's hand

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:59.080
with high accuracy.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.070
We compared the
device's output to that

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:03.950
of a Kinect placed directly
in front of the person,

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:06.110
and show that
connects up with red.

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:08.610
In comparison to Kinect,
our immediate error

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:10.910
is around 2 centimeters.

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:12.950
For more information on
how the device works,

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:15.590
check out our Project web page.

